  {
    "episode_id": 91353,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91353.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "You remember back when we used to say common sense that it was common because there'd only be a few people here and there that were just like bigger, but it really truly has become uncommon. And I don't know if maybe that is a signal of the progressive liberal mental disease, the defect, then when you start losing that common sense, but whatever did happen to that. I mean, seriously, just between the common sense, the critical thinking, I mean, even not so critical, just the basic thinking. I don't understand how Americans have lost that edge. I mean, I understand that we live in a world right now where it is instant gratification, it's bombardment with media all over the place, and I get it. But at some point, we still have to have the ability to think for ourselves. And of course, a lot of these problems are stemming from school systems that aren't teaching people, teaching the kids to actually think for themselves. And when you do think for yourselves in a lot of school systems across the country now, you end up becoming the bad guy. You end up getting kicked out of the class because you're disrupting. I saw a video not too long ago. This kid, he was a high school kid and he filmed the entire incident. And I forgot exactly what the topic was. But it had something to do with constitutional rights or something. he had questioned the teacher. And he said, wait a second, I don't think that's right. And the teacher got so mad at him for interrupting the class. Or I guess should say the lecture. And the kid was, he was actually very smart and I've watched a bunch of his other videos. But he was like, hang on a second, whoa, wait, I don't think that's accurate. That's not right. And then him and the teacher got into it back and forth about how he's interrupting the class and he needs to stop with this rhetoric and on and on. And he's like, are you kidding me? This isn't rhetoric. This is the Constitution. Are you going to read it? And he starts going on. And he got to a point where, yes, sure, it may have been quote unquote disrespectful, but it was earned. And he started looking at this teacher and saying, hey, look, if you're not going to teach us what's supposed to be teaching, you shouldn't be here. This shouldn't be what you're doing. And she's finally like, you need to leave the room because you're disrupting. And it went back and forth for a little while and the video's like about three minutes long. And finally, the kid's like, all right, fine, I'm going to leave because honestly, I don't want to be part of this. And he starts getting his stuff together. And the entire time he's getting his stuff together, he is straight up educating this teacher. And you can watch her and she's very like, you just need to get out. You're disrupting. You're a disruptor is all you are. And going on and on. And it's sad that we have teachers like that. Because in classrooms, we should have the discussion. Because remember, it's so important that we have CRT taught in the schools now so that it prepares our students for the conversation in college. I'm not kidding you. That is actually a defense of CRT. A defense for CRT is to teach the kids this in school so that they are prepared to have the conversation when they get into college. Well, why not prepare them to have a conversation now? And let's actually teach them actual history and have the conversation about history, the conversation about science, the conversation about literature. But we're getting away from that. And heaven forbid that a student who's most likely smarter than the teacher says something and confronts him and goes, wait a second. Shouldn't we talk about this? I don't think it's accurate and gets kicked out of the class. Meanwhile, the rest of the class is then brainwashed with whatever it is that they want. And we're seeing this all around the country after COVID. You know what? We've always got to find the positive in everything. And one of the biggest positives that have come out of COVID other than a positive test is the fact that parents across the country have been able to see what it is that schools have been teaching their children. It's bad for that movement, but it's good for America and good for the future of our children because now we have more parents who were forced to be woken up to this. Because look, we live in a society right now where we are busy. There's no more nine to five Monday through Friday jobs. We all know that, you know, an eight-hour-day job is just almost nonexistent nowadays. It's nine hours, 10 hours. And even when you're home, you're still on the phone. You got to check emails or whatever it might be. So time is consumed. And not enough time you'd spend with the actual family, and not enough time gets actually spent on focusing what kids are being taught. And COVID woke parents up to that. And it's a fantastic thing. It's long past due, but it's a fantastic thing. And you look at Loudon County, Virginia. Oh Lord. Those parents are about the most woke up parents ever when it comes to what's going on in the school system. And it's getting nastier too, by the way. But if we don't start teaching these kids to think for themselves and ask questions, have the conversations and do it in elementary school. You know, we've got people that jump onto these networks and they talk about how America was built on racism. Really? Where? Explain how that happened. Where did it happen? And once you explain it, let's talk about it. What was it that was so bad about slavery? How were the people treated? Let me tell you the story about it. This is what happened in early America, and we had the blacks that brought over here from Africa, and this is how they were treated. How do you feel about that? Have that conversation so that way the kids are like, oh my God, wow, you learn so much more from a conversation, but they won't do it. They just won't do it. And it's gonna be a problem for the future. And this is why we've got to tell them to knock it off. We need to be watching what it is our school boards are teaching. And we need to not put up with the media and our politicians doing what they're doing. All right, we've got to take a quick little break. We will be right back. This is the Brian Rush Show. You're listening to News Talk at 101. Good morning.",
