  {
    "episode_id": 91353,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91353.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 116,
    "turn_text": "right, welcome back to the show. Thanks so much for listening. Coming up at the top of the hour, we have Congressman Neil Duns going to be joining us on the show. I'm going to talk with him about the Build Back Better Act, Build Thing, Mess, Catastrophe, whatever it is. Along with so much more, so we'll be chatting with him coming up just a little bit to get some updates of what's going on. I have plenty of questions to ask him. So Senator Joni Erst, who's the Republican from Iowa, had proposed yesterday to axe some of the unused government programs in her latest proposal to eliminate federal waste and fraud and abuse. I can only imagine what kind of a report that has got to be in order to really truly find what is being wasted and abused and just the fraud in our government. When we have these bills, you know, right now we're facing this $3.5 trillion bill, which they're trimming down. Thank God for that. Not that it's going to matter because it's going to never end. We'll go into that in a second. But when you think about all of the money that we spend over and over and over again, and these programs that we set up, which this is one of the problems that we have, we have a problem where when you set up a program, it doesn't go away. And that is one of the dangers of what we're looking at with this Build Back Better Act. They said, oh, well, you know, okay, we're going to scale this back down to just three years instead of 15. No, it's never going to go away. Don't buy into what they're saying. Just because they say that, oh, well, you know, it's going to cost a lot less because we're only going to do it for three years. No government program only lasts that long. And as the Democrats remain, you know, just completely dedicated into wanting to pass trillions of dollars of infrastructure spending, which is really truly not even infrastructure. Most of it is not. Senator Ernst, she has actually come up with all kinds of things that she would like to see gotten rid of. She said that one of the things that she noted was the U.S. Enrichment Corporation Fund, which she said was a fund originally set up to fulfill its mission to privatize nuclear enrichment decades ago. Guess how much money this program still gets a taxpayer dollars? $1.5 billion still given to this program. The presidential election campaign fund has not supported a single candidate in five years, but has still over $400 million in idle funding. And she said that tens of millions of dollars are added to it every year. for something we're not even doing. It's just sitting there. 400 million, think about what we could do with 400 million dollars right now. Think about the roads that can be repaired. Think about the other infrastructure items that can be repaired. Think about the things that we can do with Medicare and Medicaid for those who actually need it. And of course, the Affordable Care Act requires that every state offer health insurance plans through the multi-state plan, which costs tens of millions of dollars to administer and does not provide coverage in any state due to lack of participation from any state. And yet, there's millions of dollars that are being put into this. And she goes on and on and on with all of these things. And these are programs that are just not being used. They're unused government programs. these programs never go away. And one of the things that we're facing right now when it comes to the Build Back Better Act is that, oh, well, we're only going to do it for a few years. No. You know it and I know it. It won't happen. And some of the other things that are coming to light now with the Build Back Better build, because it's taken forever for it to be read and analyzed and for the word to get out there. but it now offers a $12,500 tax rebate for electric vehicles costing up to $74,000. So if you want to be one of the 1% that actually drives and owns these cars, the Build Back Better will offer a $12,500 rebate for purchasing one. If that doesn't just get your blood boiling a little bit. I mean, first off, if you can afford a $74,000 car, you don't need a rebate. Buyers get a $4,500 tax credit if they purchase union made vehicles, putting electric car manufacturers like Tesla, Toyota, Honda at a competitive disadvantage with companies like Ford and General Motors who hire union labor. Elon Musk has previously criticized the special tax credit as a carve out for union lobbyists. Now keep that word in the back of your head, union, because there's a lot of problems with the unions right now. The bill does include price limits on electric vehicles, eligible for the tax credit, you know, 60, 74,000 dollars. Okay. But anyhow, Biden has praised the electric Ford F-150 pickup when he drove that and the electric Jeep around the White House. But the bill will provide a tax credit for even the upper middle class. And the tax credit phase out for people making over $800,000 in a filing and a tax joint return. But still, if you're making under $800,000 and you're buying a $74,000 vehicle, I mean, come on. You just got to be a little bit more responsible with your money. We don't need to be giving away all these tax discounts for that. But the Build Back Better plan doesn't stop there. Oh no. No, it doesn't. There's so much more in this. 233-9988. That is the never for the show. Good morning. Welcome to the Brian Rush Show.",
